.TH CHMAP_REDECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_redeclare \- re-establish the companion type variables for a hash map handle from another scope
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_redeclare(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares the companion type variables that carry key_t and val_t for a hash
map handle that already exists. It declares no handle, and it neither
creates nor destroys a map: it declares again only the type information
that the type-inferred macros need.

Every type-inferred macro learns the key and value types of a map from two
ordinary local variables named after the handle, which chmap_construct(3)
and chmap_declare(3) create next to the handle. Because the variables are
local declarations, they do not travel with the handle to another
function. Inside that function, chmap_insert(hm_name, ...) and the other
macros name variables that are not declared there, and the translation
unit does not compile. This macro declares them again, and every caller
that passes a chmap across a function boundary needs it.

The types here must be the types that the map was created with. The macros
use them to read a key, to size a value, and to decide whether a value is
a char * that the map stores by contents.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name of the existing map handle
key_t    Key type the map was created with
val_t    Value type the map was created with
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Expands to a sequence of declarations, so put it where a declaration is
allowed; it takes a trailing semicolon of its own
The declared variables are pointers that nothing dereferences at run
time. They exist so that typeof and the type-introspection macros can
read the types from them, and they cost nothing in the generated code
Because it declares variables only, it is also the way to widen the
reach of a handle held in a struct field or in a global: name it in each
function that works on that handle
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Do not declare a value type whose width is different from the stored one.
chmap_get(3) and chmap_get_ptr(3) report such a width at run time: they
describe the size mismatch and stop the program through ccol_fatal_err().
A mismatch between two types of the same width is not found at all, so
the types here are a promise that the caller keeps, not one that the
compiler checks.
The library reports a key of the wrong width instead of accepting it,
and the raw entry points return ccol_invalid_args for it, because the
hash of a key and its equality test have a meaning only at the width
that the map was created for. chmap_get(3) and chmap_insert(3) raise
that error to ccol_fatal_err(). chmap_get_ptr(3) and chmap_remove(3)
report it without a stop: chmap_get_ptr(3) gives a NULL result, and
chmap_remove(3) gives the return code.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void bump(chmap scores, const char *who) {
    chmap_redeclare(scores, char *, int);
    int *slot = chmap_get_ptr(scores, who);
    if (slot) {
        (*slot)++;
    }
}

chmap_construct(scores, char *, int);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
bump(scores, "alice");
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3),
.BR chmap_get (3),
.BR chmap_get_ptr (3),
.BR chmap_insert (3)
